{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Uptrend overshoot: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rally, the model tends to project too steep a continuation (especially days ~10–30), overshooting peaks by ~1–3 €/t. Apply a dampening factor to the forecast slope (e.g., reduce projected daily drift by ~20–40%).",
    "Post-spike mean reversion underestimated: after sharp upside moves (new local highs), the model often fails to revert enough and stays too high in the following 1–2 weeks. Introduce a pullback term toward a short-term mean (e.g., subtract ~0.5–1.5 €/t over days 3–10 after a spike).",
    "Turning-point lag: around reversals, the model is slow to switch direction; it remains bullish while realized prices flatten/decline. Use momentum sign-change detection (e.g., 3–5 day momentum crossing below 0) to cap further gains and tilt forecasts toward sideways/down.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small, but medium/long horizon (days 10–30) shows persistent positive bias. Apply a horizon ramp correction (e.g., 0 €/t at day 1 increasing to about -1 to -2 €/t by day 30).",
    "Volatility overprojection at long horizon: the model’s late-horizon range is too wide (predicts higher highs and deeper paths than realized). Shrink deviations from the forecast mean for days 15–30 (e.g., multiply anomalies by ~0.7–0.85)."
  ]
}
